    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1186/s12862-014-0213-0",
    "abstract": "By exploring uncertainties inherent in multiple working hypotheses, we have shown that multi-model inference is a fruitful and efficient approach to recover the nature, timing and geographical context of the Tabebuia aurea population dynamic in response to the Quaternary climate changes.",
    "field": "Phylogeography",
    "problem": "Reconstructing a species\u2019 demographic history is challenging because different processes can create similar genetic patterns, and generating realistic hypotheses is hindered by scarce fossil records and uncertainties inherent in both ecological niche and coalescent models.",
    "method": "The paper proposes a multi-model inference approach that integrates ecological niche modelling to generate alternative demographic hypotheses with coalescent simulations to test which hypothesis best explains the genetic parameters observed in modern populations.",
    "task": "The task is to recover the demographic history and distribution dynamics of a Neotropical savanna tree, *Tabebuia aurea*, by implementing a multi-model inference approach that combines ecological niche modelling with coalescent genetic simulations to understand its response to Quaternary climate changes.",
